import{_ as t,o,c as a,a2 as r}from"./chunks/framework.TMtiP8Qh.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"The payment flow","description":"Every paid request, from the 402 to the on-ledger update id — and which half of it the SDK does for you.","frontmatter":{"description":"Every paid request, from the 402 to the on-ledger update id — and which half of it the SDK does for you."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"payment-flow.md","filePath":"payment-flow.md"}'),s={name:"payment-flow.md"};function d(n,e,c,i,h,p){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="the-payment-flow" tabindex="-1">The payment flow <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-payment-flow" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The payment flow&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A paid request end to end under <code>exact</code> on Canton — from the <code>402</code> your route returns to the on-ledger update id it settles with. This is what runs underneath the route you gated in <a href="/canton-x402-sdk/pr-122/getting-started.html">Getting started</a>, and <a href="/canton-x402-sdk/pr-122/facilitator.html">The facilitator</a> then zooms in on steps 5 and 6. You write almost none of it: the merchant side (steps 1, 5 and 6) is handled via <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@chainsafe/x402-express" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>@chainsafe/x402-express</code></a>, and the payer side (steps 2–4) via the payer&#39;s own SDK, <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@chainsafe/x402-server-sdk" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>@chainsafe/x402-server-sdk</code></a>. You configure two things yourself: your route&#39;s price and terms in the <code>x402-express</code> middleware (step 1), and the max header size on your resource server (step 4).</p><ol><li><p><strong>Client hits a gated route (402)</strong></p><p>Your route answers <code>402 Payment Required</code> with the spec&#39;s <code>PaymentRequired</code>, <code>{ x402Version: 2, resource, accepts: [...] }</code>, base64-encoded in the <code>PAYMENT-REQUIRED</code> header and as the body. <code>resource</code> names what is being paid for: the request URL and the route&#39;s description. Each <code>accepts</code> entry carries <code>scheme</code>, <code>network</code>, <code>amount</code>, <code>asset</code>, <code>extra.assetTransferMethod</code>, <code>extra.instrumentId</code>, <code>extra.executeBeforeSeconds</code> (120 by default, and optionally an <code>extra.memo</code>), <code>payTo</code> (your Canton party) and a <code>maxTimeoutSeconds</code> (60 by default).</p></li><li><p><strong>Payer builds the transfer</strong></p><p>The payer queries its Amulet holdings and fetches a <code>TransferFactory</code> from the Splice Token Standard registry for the asset — the validator scan-proxy for Amulet.</p></li><li><p><strong>Prepare and sign</strong></p><p>The payer calls Canton&#39;s <code>interactive-submission/prepare</code> to get the transaction blob and its hash, then Ed25519-signs that hash with its party key. Nothing is executed, and the private key never leaves the payer.</p></li><li><p><strong>Retry with <code>PAYMENT-SIGNATURE</code></strong></p><p>The payment — the requirements it was built against as <code>accepted</code> (the <code>accepts[]</code> entry the payer chose), and the payload: <code>assetTransferMethod</code>, <code>preparedTransaction</code>, <code>preparedTxHash</code>, <code>signature</code>, <code>publicKey</code>, <code>hashingSchemeVersion</code> — is JSON-encoded, base64&#39;d into a <code>PAYMENT-SIGNATURE</code> header, and the request is retried. That header carries the prepared transaction, gzipped, with its disclosed contracts. The middleware also reads a payment of this shape sent under <code>X-PAYMENT</code>, the name this SDK used before.</p></li><li><p><strong>Merchant verifies, then settles</strong></p><p>Your middleware re-checks the payment against your own terms — scheme, network, <code>payTo</code>, asset, <code>maxTimeoutSeconds</code>, and that it is neither underpriced nor expired — using the exact requirements the payment was built against, its <code>accepted</code>, which the facilitator then holds the signed transfer to, so a payment built for other terms can&#39;t pass. It then POSTs <code>{ x402Version, paymentPayload, paymentRequirements }</code> to <code>/v2/verify</code> and, with <code>settle: true</code> (the default is <code>false</code>: verify only), to <code>/v2/settle</code>, with your API key. A payment that fails your terms or the facilitator&#39;s verify gets a fresh challenge (a new <code>402</code>) instead of being settled.</p></li><li><p><strong>Facilitator executes on Canton</strong></p><p><code>/v2/settle</code> re-runs <a href="/canton-x402-sdk/pr-122/facilitator.html#what-verify-guarantees">every verify check</a>, then calls Canton&#39;s <code>executeAndWaitForTransaction</code> with the payer-signed blob. On success it returns the update id as <code>transaction</code>, which the middleware puts on your response as <code>X-PAYMENT-RESPONSE</code>, and your handler runs with the verified payer on <code>req.x402</code>.</p></li></ol><div class="danger custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Raise your server&#39;s max header size</p><p>The <code>PAYMENT-SIGNATURE</code> header from step 4 runs to about 10 KB for a typical transfer — much of Node&#39;s ~16 KB default, which covers all of a request&#39;s headers together — so a Node/Express merchant server should set <code>maxHeaderSize</code> (e.g. <code>--max-http-header-size</code>) or the paid retry dies with <code>431 Request Header Fields Too Large</code> before your handler ever runs. Step 6 of <a href="/canton-x402-sdk/pr-122/getting-started.html#wire-up-your-server">Getting started</a> shows where it goes. The scheme spec asks every server and proxy on the way to accept at least 16 KiB for this header. A proxy in front needs it too: nginx&#39;s default <code>large_client_header_buffers 4 8k</code> refuses any single header over 8 KiB.</p></div>',4)])])}const f=t(s,[["render",d]]);export{u as __pageData,f as default};
